1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Part 3

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:09,000
You will hear two students called Jean and Thomas discussing their sports science course.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,000
First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 24.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 24.

5
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Hi Thomas, how are you enjoying the course so far?

6
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Yeah, I think it's good.

7
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Remind me, why did you decide to study sports science?

8
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Didn't you want to be a professional athlete when you were at school?

9
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Yeah, that was my goal and all my classmates assumed I would achieve it.

10
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
They thought I was brilliant.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,000
That must have been a nice feeling.

12
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,000
I thought I could win anything.

13
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
There was no one who could run faster than me.

14
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Exactly, so what happened?

15
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Did your mum and dad want you to be more academic?

16
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Not at all.

17
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Perhaps they should have pushed me harder though.

18
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:19,000
What do you mean?

19
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
I think I should have practiced more.

20
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,000
What makes you say that?

21
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Well, I went out to Kenya for a couple of weeks to train.

22
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Really? I didn't know that.

23
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:36,000
I was chosen to go there out of loads of kids and run with some of the top teenage athletes in the world.

24
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
And I was so calm about it.

25
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,000
I just kept thinking how fortunate I was.

26
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,000
What a great chance this was.

27
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Everyone back home was so proud of me.

28
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
But once we started competing, I very quickly realised I wasn't good enough.

29
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,000
That must have been a huge shock.

30
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
I thought this can't be happening. I was used to winning.

31
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:01,000
I'm sorry to hear that.

32
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:07,000
It's okay. I'm over it now and I think it's much better to do a university course.

33
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:12,000
And this one has such a variety of sports related areas. It's going to be good.

34
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Well, I agree. I chose it because of that.

35
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 25 to 30.

36
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30.

37
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,000
So, Jian, have you thought of any ideas for the discussion session next week on technology and sport?

38
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
We have to cover more than one sport, don't we?

39
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Yeah.

40
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
You know, we always think technology is about the future.

41
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
But we could gather some ideas about past developments in sport.

42
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Look at early types of equipment, perhaps.

43
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:33,000
I remember reading something about table tennis bats once, how they ended up being covered with pimpled rubber.

44
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Because they were just wooden at first, I'd imagine.

45
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Yeah. In about the 1920s, a factory was making rolls of the rubber in bulk for something like horse harnesses.

46
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:45,000
Really?

47
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Yeah. And someone realised that it would make a perfect covering for the wooden bats.

48
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:55,000
So, what about cricket? That's had a few innovative changes.

49
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Maybe the pads they wear on their legs?

50
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
I don't think they've changed much, but I'm just looking on the internet.

51
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:08,000
And it says that when the first cricket helmet came in, in 1978,

52
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:15,000
the Australian batsman who first wore it was booed and jeered by people watching because it was so ugly.

53
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Wow. Players have to protect themselves from getting hurt. I mean, everyone wears one now.

54
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,000
Hmm. Unlike the cycle helmet.

55
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:32,000
Well, unless you're a professional, but you're right, many ordinary bikers don't wear a helmet.

56
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Hey, look at these pictures of original helmet designs.

57
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
This one looks like an upside down bowl.

58
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Yet the woman's laughing. She's so proud to be wearing it.

59
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:50,000
It says serious cyclists ended up with wet hair from all the hard exercise.

60
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:56,000
I guess that's why they have large air vents in them now, so that the skin can breathe more easily.

61
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Okay, so we've done helmets.

62
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:05,000
What about golf balls? Or better still, golf clubs. They've changed a lot.

63
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Yeah. I remember my great grandfather telling me that because a club was made entirely of wood,

64
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,000
it would easily break and players had to get another.

65
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
There's no wood at all in them now, is there?

66
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
No. They're much more powerful.

67
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
The same must be true of hockey sticks.

68
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Hmm. I don't think so because players still use wooden sticks today.

69
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:36,000
Hmm. What it does say here, though, is that when the game started, you had to produce a stick yourself.

70
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,000
I guess they just weren't being manufactured.

71
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,000
So, one more perhaps. What about football?

72
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Well, I know the first balls were made of animal skin.

73
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Yeah. They covered them with pieces of leather that were stitched together, but the balls let in water when it rained.

74
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Oh, that would have made them much heavier.

75
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:04,000
That's right. You can imagine the damage to players' necks when the ball was headed.

76
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Oh, how painful that must have been.

77
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Yeah. Well, I think we can put together some useful ideas.

78
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:21,000
That is the end of Part 3. You now have half a minute to check your answers to Part 3.

79
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Thank you.

